256
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:04,360
that could fit in the palm of your hand.

257
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:06,740
This unit that you see right in the middle here

258
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:10,280
is what we call our BSA, or our balance sensor assembly.

259
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:15,480
These components work together to figure out which way is up

260
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:19,080
and figure out when the platform is level.

261
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:21,780
This balancing assembly is a vast network

262
00:16:21,781 --> 00:16:26,780
of wires and sensors, inspired by the human body.

263
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:30,780
A person's equilibrium comes from the function of the inner ear,

264
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:34,220
which contains tiny patches of hair cells.

265
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:37,420
When the head moves, the cells are stimulated,

266
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:40,540
causing them to send messages to the brain.

267
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:44,240
The brain then tells the body where it is in relation

268
00:16:44,340 --> 00:16:48,140
to gravity and Earth in order for it to remain balanced.

269
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:51,680
In the Segway, sensors respond to the right

270
00:16:51,681 --> 00:16:56,180
shift and wait. Signals are then sent to the processor, or the

271
00:16:56,181 --> 00:17:00,120
brain of the Segway. From there, operational commands go

272
00:17:00,121 --> 00:17:03,220
to the motors, which adjust the wheels to keep the platform

273
00:17:03,221 --> 00:17:09,440
level. The result? A fluid and seamless

274
00:17:09,441 --> 00:17:14,980
riding experience. With the balance in place, one

275
00:17:14,981 --> 00:17:20,440
question still remains. How fast does it go?

276
00:17:20,441 --> 00:17:23,780
The Segway PTs can go up to 12 and 1 half miles an hour,

277
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:27,180
which is about three to four times a walking pace.

278
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:31,020
It's roughly the equivalent of an average person sprinting.

279
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:33,080
And the reason we chose 12 and 1 half miles an hour

280
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:35,060
was because we designed the Segway PT to fit

281
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:38,600
into a pedestrian environment, so operating on sidewalks

282
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:40,120
with other people who might be walking.

283
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:43,600
So we chose that speed limit so that the vehicle would integrate

284
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:44,960
more easily into that environment.

285
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:50,200
Because of its smooth glide,

286
00:17:50,201 --> 00:17:54,200
The Segway PT has been adopted to alleviate a different set

287
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:55,200
of commuting obstacles.

288
00:17:58,200 --> 00:18:01,340
SEGS for Vets is an organization that is utilizing

289
00:18:01,440 --> 00:18:04,940
the unit's intuitive technology to help veterans who have

290
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:08,360
sustained catastrophic injuries during combat operations.

291
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:09,560
Yeah, very nice.

292
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:10,460
Very nice.

293
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:12,760
What's great is that through legislation

294
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:16,660
that we've been able to pass, the Segway is now considered

295
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:17,960
a fully fledged wheelchair.

296
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:25,200
A group of recipients are being trained on how to best navigate

297
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:28,200
the Segway PT through their new set of challenges.

298
00:18:35,140 --> 00:18:38,260
SEGS4Vets has donated over 500 Segway units

299
00:18:38,360 --> 00:18:43,660
to eligible veterans, an unintended benefit

300
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:46,960
born from the very technology Dean Kamen created

301
00:18:47,060 --> 00:18:50,200
to help those with disabilities.

302
00:18:50,201 --> 00:18:51,860
It changes everything about it,

303
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:53,540
and you don't mind being on a Segway.

304
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:55,200
You don't feel disabled, and people

305
00:18:55,300 --> 00:18:56,440
don't look at you as disabled.

306
00:18:56,540 --> 00:19:00,140
They're always just looking up at you now.

307
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:04,740
The Segway just became an extension of my legs.

308
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:07,720
And it's given me back a lot of things

309
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:08,860
that I thought I'd lost.

310
00:19:08,861 --> 00:19:22,700
This balanced technology is also being exported.

311
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:25,680
Marathon Robotics has applied the technology

312
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:29,640
in creating live fire exercises for the Australian military.

313
00:19:33,660 --> 00:19:37,860
A teleoperator can control a fleet of free-ranging targets

314
00:19:37,861 --> 00:19:41,500
by designing a game scenario on a computer.

315
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:43,860
This allows the conductor to simulate

316
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:48,800
unpredictable movements for the soldier's point of view.

317
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,280
But in the broader scope, Segway's goal

318
00:19:54,380 --> 00:19:58,840
is still to help the world rethink the way it commutes.

319
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:02,460
The next generation of US-made Segway transporters

320
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:06,620
has been unveiled, such as the Puma.

321
00:20:06,621 --> 00:20:09,760
Utilizing the Segway sensory technology,

322
00:20:09,860 --> 00:20:14,200
this transporter steers with a drive-by wire interface,

323
00:20:14,300 --> 00:20:18,600
eliminating all mechanical controls.

324
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:21,540
Another is the Envy, which is designed

325
00:20:21,640 --> 00:20:25,100
to perform autonomous mobility, leaving the driving

326
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:28,040
to the machine.

327
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:30,380
In collaboration with General Motors,

328
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:36,620
these visionary prototypes are slated for a 2030 release.

329
00:20:36,621 --> 00:20:39,860
The Segway PT is an American-made machine that

330
00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:44,560
defies conventional design and a company that pushes forward

331
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:47,660
in creating the transportation of the future today.

332
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:54,880
Being made in America isn't only

333
00:20:54,980 --> 00:20:57,580
about the textile and electronic industries.

334
00:20:57,680 --> 00:20:59,980
Our food has also reached the global market.

335
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:05,980
Discovery Foods in Hayward, California,

336
00:21:05,981 --> 00:21:08,680
has become one of the largest frozen food distributors

337
00:21:08,780 --> 00:21:12,180
in the country.

338
00:21:12,280 --> 00:21:16,020
And this American company has become the top provider

339
00:21:16,120 --> 00:21:21,420
of a very popular Asian appetizer, the potsticker.

340
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:24,040
Also known as a dumpling, the potsticker

341
00:21:24,140 --> 00:21:29,440
consists of a meat or vegetable filling thinly wrapped in dough.

342
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:32,080
A dumpling is traditionally boiled,

343
00:21:32,180 --> 00:21:33,480
where the potsticker is fried.

344
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:41,460
Our Ling Ling potstickers are the leading parts to potstickers in the world.

345
00:21:41,461 --> 00:21:46,080
We have the largest market share of anyone in the United States, well recognized as the

346
00:21:46,081 --> 00:21:52,080
authentic great tasting potsticker to give the real Asian experience with the convenience

347
00:21:52,081 --> 00:21:56,740
of home preparation.

348
00:21:56,741 --> 00:21:59,860
This is the beginning of our process in our mixing and grinding room.

349
00:21:59,861 --> 00:22:05,220
We process roughly 25,000 pounds of chicken a day.

350
00:22:05,221 --> 00:22:17,360
10 tons of cabbage and enough flour in the process to make 15,000 loaves of bread every day.

351
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:23,600
We bring in our raw chicken, grind it, mix it with the cabbage,

352
00:22:26,440 --> 00:22:27,760
spices and oils.

353
00:22:27,761 --> 00:22:34,760
The ingredients are finely blended together to reach a

354
00:22:34,761 --> 00:22:36,760
consistent filling.

355
00:22:36,761 --> 00:22:39,760
At the same time, the dough ingredients are mixed, then

356
00:22:39,761 --> 00:22:43,760
threaded to a ribbon to flatten out the dumpling skin.

357
00:22:43,761 --> 00:22:47,760
The remaining scraps of dough are collected, then recycled back

358
00:22:47,761 --> 00:22:51,760
through the ribbon to create more dumpling circles, wasting as

359
00:22:51,761 --> 00:22:55,760
few grains of flour as possible.

360
00:22:55,761 --> 00:22:58,760
Now it's time to create the dimpled dumplings.

361
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:06,600
The dough is cut into circles, and the dumplings are loaded

362
00:23:06,700 --> 00:23:09,100
with 12 and 1 1 grams of filling.

363
00:23:09,200 --> 00:23:12,520
The automated process that we use assures uniformity

364
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:16,020
of the skin and consistency of the filler.

365
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:19,420
By selling the calibration of each of the machines,

366
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:22,260
we know precisely how thick the skin is,

367
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:24,520
how much filler is in each top sticker,

368
00:23:24,521 --> 00:23:27,760
potsticker and what the total weight of each unit is.

369
00:23:27,761 --> 00:23:31,520
Our potsticker line produces more than 25 potstickers per

370
00:23:31,521 --> 00:23:34,960
second, or over a million potstickers every day.

371
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:43,000
They are then pinched with stainless steel fingers

372
00:23:43,001 --> 00:23:46,440
to mimic the look and feel of a dumpling that was made by hand.

373
00:23:46,441 --> 00:23:53,440
The pot stickers then head to the freeze process

374
00:23:53,441 --> 00:23:55,440
to lock in the freshness.

375
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:02,440
This is the spiral freezer, which preserves the products

376
00:24:02,441 --> 00:24:06,440
so that they are wholesome and great tasting for our consumers.

377
00:24:06,441 --> 00:24:08,440
In this freezer, we maintain the temperature

378
00:24:08,441 --> 00:24:10,440
at minus 14 degrees Fahrenheit.

379
00:24:10,441 --> 00:24:13,440
The product resides in here, moving into the freezer

380
00:24:13,441 --> 00:24:17,280
The product resides in here, moving up the spiral

381
00:24:17,380 --> 00:24:20,840
for a period of 23 minutes, at which time it is completely

382
00:24:20,940 --> 00:24:24,080
frozen and ready for our bagging and packaging operation

383
00:24:24,180 --> 00:24:27,380
to ship to our consumer.

384
00:24:27,480 --> 00:24:31,100
This American phenomenon of buy now, eat later

385
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:34,000
allows highly perishable foods to be distributed

386
00:24:34,100 --> 00:24:37,640
all over the world without compromising its freshness

387
00:24:37,740 --> 00:24:41,940
or taste for months to come.

388
00:24:41,941 --> 00:24:45,940
The frozen food industry can be credited to New York taxidermist

389
00:24:46,040 --> 00:24:50,480
Clarence Birdseye, whose 1923 quick freeze method

390
00:24:50,580 --> 00:24:54,880
revolutionized food in the US.

391
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:57,880
This technique works by quickly exposing foods

392
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:00,800
to extremely low temperatures.

393
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:04,900
In accelerating the freeze time, fewer ice crystals form,

394
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,600
which can damage tissue structure.

395
00:25:07,700 --> 00:25:09,940
As a result, the fresh taste remains

396
00:25:09,941 --> 00:25:11,940
until the consumer is ready to eat.

397
00:25:16,740 --> 00:25:19,940
The frozen potstickers work their way over to the weighing

398
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:22,580
and packaging area.

399
00:25:22,680 --> 00:25:25,940
An elaborate system of computerized scales is used to

400
00:25:26,040 --> 00:25:29,000
weigh out one bag of potstickers.

401
00:25:29,100 --> 00:25:32,000
They pass through several weigh buckets in order to wean down

402
00:25:32,100 --> 00:25:34,000
any excess pieces.

403
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:37,000
Once the buckets approve the weight, the potstickers are

404
00:25:37,001 --> 00:25:40,020
Pot stickers are combined for a final portion calculation,

405
00:25:40,800 --> 00:25:42,160
then dropped into the bag.

406
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:49,080
At this rate, the packaging line seals over 52,000 bags a day.

407
00:25:50,920 --> 00:25:54,020
Another million pot stickers heads to stores.

408
00:25:55,960 --> 00:25:58,900
And this bubbled appetizer is ready for you

409
00:25:58,901 --> 00:26:01,020
whenever you are ready for it.

410
00:26:03,840 --> 00:26:06,540
In discovering all things made in the USA,

411
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,400
We ran the gamut with New Balance,

412
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:13,140
blazed a trail with Segway, and indulged an American treat

413
00:26:13,240 --> 00:26:16,240
with an Asian twist at Discovery Foods.

414
00:26:16,340 --> 00:26:18,980
But to understand the innovations of today,

415
00:26:19,080 --> 00:26:22,420
you have to reach back to the inspirations of yesterday,

416
00:26:22,520 --> 00:26:25,120
toys.

417
00:26:25,220 --> 00:26:27,920
K'nex in Hatfield, Pennsylvania,

418
00:26:28,020 --> 00:26:31,060
is the only large-scale toy manufacturing company

419
00:26:31,160 --> 00:26:33,060
in the United States.

420
00:26:33,160 --> 00:26:36,560
By thinking outside of the box, this US company

421
00:26:36,561 --> 00:26:40,360
now sells over 10 million sets a year.

422
00:26:40,361 --> 00:26:45,360
Started in 1992 in just 500 U.S. retail stores,

423
00:26:45,361 --> 00:26:49,500
K'nex toys are now carried in 17,000 stores

424
00:26:49,501 --> 00:26:52,200
in over 40 countries.

425
00:26:52,201 --> 00:26:54,120
The concept is simple.

426
00:26:54,121 --> 00:26:59,420
Take a rod and connector, snap them together, and repeat.

427
00:26:59,421 --> 00:27:03,820
These basic parts allow for an infinite amount of possibilities

428
00:27:03,821 --> 00:27:06,820
and have spawned a new generation of K'nex toys.

429
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:12,820
The modern-day toy factory consists of an army

430
00:27:12,920 --> 00:27:16,460
of stainless steel, high-tech machinery.

431
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:19,660
K'nex has over 250,000 square feet

432
00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:24,980
of manufacturing automation, working 24 hours a day,

433
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:27,380
seven days a week.

434
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:29,520
Our manufacturing process is known

435
00:27:29,620 --> 00:27:31,480
as plastic injection molding.

436
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:37,460
Essentially, we're making fancy jello molds in stainless steel

437
00:27:37,461 --> 00:27:39,460
to exactly whatever part it is.

438
00:27:41,720 --> 00:27:45,140
When a newly designed part comes in from the development team,

439
00:27:45,620 --> 00:27:47,940
a stainless steel cavity must be created.

440
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:52,000
Using state-of-the-art mold fabrication precision,

441
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:55,060
lasers electronically burn out the cavity,

442
00:27:55,620 --> 00:28:00,240
which is to mirror the design of the rod, connector, or brick.

443
00:28:00,241 --> 00:28:06,240
Outdoor silos hold over 200,000 pounds of petroleum-based plastic

444
00:28:06,241 --> 00:28:10,240
before the material is brought in to be melted and molded

445
00:28:10,241 --> 00:28:12,240
into a part.

446
00:28:12,241 --> 00:28:14,240
The basic injection molding process

447
00:28:14,241 --> 00:28:18,240
is the taking of a solid plastic, feeding it up

448
00:28:18,241 --> 00:28:21,240
into the proportional loader, mixing it with color,

449
00:28:21,241 --> 00:28:24,240
and converting it into a liquid at a very high temperature,

450
00:28:24,241 --> 00:28:29,240
in this case, 420 degrees, which will come out of the nozzle

451
00:28:29,241 --> 00:28:32,540
and shoot into the back of the tool

452
00:28:32,541 --> 00:28:35,240
and actually push the molten plastic

453
00:28:35,241 --> 00:28:37,240
into the cavities of the tool

454
00:28:37,241 --> 00:28:39,880
to form the impression of that cavity,

455
00:28:39,881 --> 00:28:42,880
which in this case is a K'nex rod.

456
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:50,400
Each press is microprocessor controlled

457
00:28:50,401 --> 00:28:53,240
and self-diagnosed with a clamping force

458
00:28:53,241 --> 00:28:56,240
of over 400 tons.

459
00:28:56,241 --> 00:28:59,840
With over 5 billion parts made each year,

460
00:28:59,940 --> 00:29:02,840
K'nex is the only major U.S. manufacturer

461
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:07,180
able to compete with China's grip on toy production.

462
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:09,780
The reason we're able to compete with everybody

463
00:29:09,880 --> 00:29:12,860
around the world with our parts is because we've automated

464
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:14,900
and invested in incredible technology

465
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,500
to get the precision of the parts every time.

466
00:29:17,600 --> 00:29:20,940
So every one of these 5 billion parts that we make every year,

467
00:29:21,040 --> 00:29:23,640
the parts are better, the parts are less expensive,

468
00:29:23,641 --> 00:29:25,640
and they're closer to our customers.

469
00:29:25,641 --> 00:29:29,640
It's what Made in America is all about.

470
00:29:29,641 --> 00:29:31,640
K'nex is known for its big ideas,

471
00:29:31,641 --> 00:29:33,640
such as working grandfather clocks,

472
00:29:33,641 --> 00:29:37,640
bikes you can ride, to even a fully functional 8,000-piece

473
00:29:37,641 --> 00:29:38,640
Ferris wheel.

474
00:29:38,641 --> 00:29:42,640
It'll take a good builder anywhere from 15 to 20 hours

475
00:29:42,641 --> 00:29:45,640
to put together, and definitely with this many pieces,

476
00:29:45,641 --> 00:29:48,640
you can then build whatever you imagine.

477
00:29:50,640 --> 00:29:51,640
The system is infinite.

478
00:29:51,641 --> 00:29:53,140
The system is infinite.

479
00:29:53,141 --> 00:29:55,440
You can really build anything you want.

480
00:29:55,441 --> 00:29:58,040
But we know we just can't sell one single box of toys.

481
00:29:58,041 --> 00:30:01,540
So we do prescribed building, whether it's a roller coaster,

482
00:30:01,541 --> 00:30:03,180
a car, an airplane.

483
00:30:03,181 --> 00:30:07,480
We want kids to buy multiple K'nex toys that can all work together.

484
00:30:07,481 --> 00:30:08,800
It's a big bin at home.

485
00:30:08,801 --> 00:30:11,300
Once you get more, you dump them in and you just keep growing

486
00:30:11,301 --> 00:30:13,300
and growing the bin of K'nex parts

487
00:30:13,301 --> 00:30:15,240
so you can build anything you want.

488
00:30:15,241 --> 00:30:18,240
From the classics to the confusing,

489
00:30:18,241 --> 00:30:21,000
no idea is too simple or perplexing

490
00:30:21,001 --> 00:30:24,600
development team at K'nex.

491
00:30:24,700 --> 00:30:27,500
This group spends countless hours working on creating the

492
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:31,640
next great toy set or model.

493
00:30:31,740 --> 00:30:34,840
The design process starts in a couple of different ways.

494
00:30:34,940 --> 00:30:38,520
We take an idea and we do what we call a sketch.

495
00:30:38,620 --> 00:30:41,780
And that sketch starts either on the computer or with the

496
00:30:41,880 --> 00:30:45,060
parts that we have and do a physical sketch.

497
00:30:45,160 --> 00:30:48,160
We then take that sketch and show it to the design team

498
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:51,000
and the marketing team and sometimes focus groups of

499
00:30:51,001 --> 00:30:55,240
as well and see if that sketch has some legs to it.

500
00:30:55,340 --> 00:30:58,200
Once those legs are built, the toy needs to be carefully

501
00:30:58,300 --> 00:31:02,340
dissected and mapped out, because the construction of the set is

502
00:31:02,440 --> 00:31:05,140
all part of the fun.

503
00:31:05,240 --> 00:31:08,860
Graphic designers are given the task to devise steps that pose

504
00:31:08,960 --> 00:31:12,560
a challenge for the assembler, but at the same time,

505
00:31:12,660 --> 00:31:15,460
maintaining a user-friendly appeal.

506
00:31:15,560 --> 00:31:17,560
We spend about half of our development dollars

507
00:31:17,561 --> 00:31:21,360
